/**
 * CONSTRAINT IDENTIFICATION
 *   constraint_id: human_dignity_ai_governance__magisterial_integralist_reading
 *   human_readable: Catholic Social Doctrine Authority in AI Governance (Magisterial Integralist Reading)
 *   domain: theological/political/technological
 *
 * SUMMARY:
 *   This constraint instantiates the Magisterial Integralist reading of the
 *   human dignity / AI governance kernel. The reading claims that human
 *   dignity is an ontological gift from God (imago Dei), infinite and
 *   inalienable, knowable through both faith and reason. From this
 *   foundation, it asserts that AI governance must conform to Catholic Social
 *   Doctrine principles as interpreted by the Magisterium (the official
 *   teaching authority of the Catholic Church), and that the Church holds
 *   unique authority to guide technological development toward the common
 *   good. The constraint structures AI design toward relational anthropology,
 *   embodied personhood, and finite transcendence — in opposition to
 *   transhumanist projects that treat the human body as substrate for
 *   optimization and to technocratic elites who treat AI governance as a
 *   technical problem decoupled from metaphysical anthropology. This reading
 *   coexists in the kernel contest with secular-humanist (dignity grounded in
 *   rational autonomy and universal rights), pluralist-pragmatic (dignity as
 *   contested concept requiring negotiated frameworks), and techno-optimist
 *   (dignity enhanced through augmentation) readings. This JSON authors ONLY
 *   the Magisterial reading; sibling readings are separate constraint
 *   stories.
 *
 * KEY AGENTS:
 *   - magisterial_authority (Church teaching office): sets doctrine on imago Dei and derives AI governance requirements
 *   - vulnerable_populations (poor, workers, disabled, marginalized): benefit from protections grounded in relational dignity and worker rights
 *   - families (as relational unit grounded in imago Dei): benefit from design constraints that protect embodied social structures
 *   - technocratic_elites (Silicon Valley, algorithmic-systems engineers, transhumanist theorists): victims of the constraint's prohibition on certain research directions and design paradigms
 *   - transhumanist_projects (radical life extension, cognitive enhancement, uploading): targeted by the constraint's assertion of intrinsic dignity in finite embodied human nature
 *   - secular_authorities (democratic governments, regulatory bodies, secular NGOs): excluded from Magisterial authority claim but invited to recognize overlapping-consensus elements
 *   - dissenting_catholics (theologians, lay organizations, bishops in pluralist countries): present in the constraint's implementation but not in its authority structure; relationship is contested
 */

